WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.688
For the examples in this module we use a small
synthetic healthcare

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:09.838
operations database The data is fictional and
designed only for practice There

00:00:09.862 --> 00:00:14.425
are four main tables The patients table contains
information such as a

00:00:14.438 --> 00:00:18.650
medical record number a patient name age gender and
whether the patient

00:00:18.662 --> 00:00:24.375
is active The doctors table stores doctor
information including names and specialties

00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:29.362
The admissions table connects patients and doctors
with information such as diagnosis

00:00:29.525 --> 00:00:35.438
admission date discharge date and status The
clinical_notes table stores patient-linked

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.338
notes in JSONB format which gives us a chance to
work with

00:00:38.362 --> 00:00:44.450
semi-structured data later in the module These
tables are intentionally small A

00:00:44.462 --> 00:00:47.812
small dataset makes it easier to compare your SQL
statement with the

00:00:47.825 --> 00:00:52.125
result and understand why a row was included or
excluded You may

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:57.012
notice that the patients table stores age directly
Real systems often store

00:00:57.025 --> 00:01:00.438
a date of birth instead because age changes over
time and depends

00:01:00.462 --> 00:01:03.925
on the reference date We keep age as a column here
only

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:08.488
to make our first SQL examples easier to read The
important idea

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.538
is that the same SQL patterns you learn with this
small database

00:01:11.562 --> 00:01:15.588
can be applied to many other domains such as retail
education finance

00:01:15.762 --> 00:01:17.025
or business operations
